Understood — no customer accounts, just you signing into `/admin`. Here's the login path end to end. I verified each step against the actual code in the zip.

**Why it doesn't work right now:** `AdminLoginPage.js` calls `supabase.auth.signInWithPassword(...)`, then queries the `admins` table and signs you out again if no row matches your user ID. So you need three things: an auth user with a known password, an `admins` row for it, and the app's env vars. The zip is missing `supabase-schema.sql`, so the table likely doesn't exist yet — I've written the SQL below.

---

**Step 1 — Get your API keys**

Supabase dashboard → **Project Settings → API**. Copy the **Project URL** and the **anon public** key. Create a file called `.env.local` next to `package.json`:

```
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxx.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGci...
```

`.gitignore` already excludes `.env.local`, so it won't be committed. If you deploy to Vercel, add the same two variables there under Settings → Environment Variables and redeploy. Without them the app logs "Missing Supabase env vars" and every query fails.

**Step 2 — Create an admin user with a password you know**

Dashboard → **Authentication → Users → Add user**. Enter your email, set a password, and tick **Auto-confirm user**.

If a user already exists from your earlier reset-email attempt and you don't know its password, just **delete it and re-add it** — since you're the only user, that's faster than any reset flow. (This is also why you can ignore the localhost reset link entirely.)

Copy the **UID** from that user's row — you'll need it next.

**Step 3 — Create the `admins` table with its security policy**

Dashboard → **SQL Editor → New query**, paste this, then **Run**:

```sql
create table if not exists public.admins (
  id uuid primary key references auth.users(id) on delete cascade,
  email text,
  created_at timestamptz default now()
);

alter table public.admins enable row level security;

drop policy if exists "admin can read own row" on public.admins;
create policy "admin can read own row"
  on public.admins for select
  to authenticated
  using (auth.uid() = id);
```

This is the whole security model: a signed-in user can only see their own row, and the row's existence is what makes you an admin. Without it the login form will accept your password and then reject you with "This account is not authorised for admin access."

**Step 4 — Insert your admin row**

Still in the SQL Editor:

```sql
insert into public.admins (id, email)
select id, email from auth.users
where lower(email) = 'you@example.com'   -- <-- your real email
on conflict (id) do nothing;
```

Or do it by hand: **Table Editor → admins → Insert row**, setting `id` to the UID you copied and `email` to your email.

**Step 5 — Sign in**

Open **`/admin/login`** on your site (locally that's `http://localhost:3000/admin/login` after `npm install` and `npm run dev`). Enter your email and password. You land on `/admin`.

